[{:seon.task/name "one-reference-reader"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists (docs/references.md): `seon.ref`, v1's reference reader (~/src/seon/src/seon/ref.clj, its tests ~/src/seon/test/seon/ref_test.clj, the rule in ~/src/seon/docs/seon/schedule/cards/TASK-CHAIN.md §3a) on next's principles. One Markdown parse of any text (a plan, a doc page, a docstring), `seon.ref/parse`:
- the delimiter picks the depth: a bare word that names a real entity is its summary; one backtick or a link is its doc; three or more backticks is its code; two backticks is a literal and never a reference;
- a name cited several times is shown once, at its strongest depth (`seon.ref/cited`);
- a line citation (`src/seon/writer.clj:263`) is its file plus `:seon.ref/problem-kind :line-reference`; whether that is red is `seon.docs/the-wiki-stays-true-and-healthy`'s call;
- a section's summary is its heading plus its lead's first sentence.
Names resolve against the program facts (`:var/name`). The Markdown is a minimum exact scanner for the CommonMark subset the rule needs (ATX headings with GitHub-style anchors, ``` and ~~~ fences, code spans by the equal-length backtick rule, inline links, paragraph leads), proven by v1's ported tests and 70 CommonMark spec examples. One parse returns sections (address, heading, lead, parent) and citations (what, depth, line); it costs 0.8 ms and 1.2 MB on AGENTS.md, two thirds of it `seon.reader/read-text` per candidate name. Replaced `seon.reconcile/references` (backtick-only) in the context's current state, `hairy` and the docs check; dropped v1's cache, sci and embedding hooks. There is no declared reference list anywhere: references are only what text cites."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ref-test/one-text-gives-names-paths-and-line-citations-by-one-rule
                      seon.ref-test/the-delimiter-picks-the-depth
                      seon.ref-test/a-literal-and-fenced-code-are-not-references
                      seon.ref-test/a-bare-word-is-a-reference-only-when-it-names-something
                      seon.ref-test/a-name-is-shown-once-at-its-strongest-depth
                      seon.ref-test/a-line-citation-is-its-file-marked-line-reference
                      seon.ref-test/commonmark-examples-for-the-subset
                      seon.ref-test/a-section-summary-is-its-heading-and-leads-first-sentence
                      seon.ref-test/one-parse-costs-one-pass}}
 {:seon.task/name "docs-are-data-in-the-store"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists (docs/references.md#the-wiki-in-the-store): the wiki is loaded into main as `:seon.section/fact` entities (resources/seon/schemas/seon.section.edn), one per heading, addressed by the parse's `path#anchor` (`docs/flow.md#idioms`, the form links resolve to), with title, lead, own text, parent and references (what its text cites, read by `seon.ref/parse`: links to sections, names to var entities). Written by `seon.docs/load-tx` (`seon.docs/load-dir-tx` over `seon.docs/pages`), read by `seon.docs/section` and `seon.docs/view`; loaded at boot (`seon.root/wiki-tx`) and on demand (`my.root/load-docs!`). No page digest is stored: a reload compares each section's stated attributes with main's, so an unchanged wiki answers [] at the cost of a parse and a pull per section (19 ms, 23 MB for docs/ when measured). A section's backlinks are the sections that cite it. Not stored: file entities (so file citations and schema keys) and unresolved names (left to `seon.docs/the-wiki-stays-true-and-healthy`). A doc change on a branch merges like any entity keyed by its identity (`:seon.section/address`); no test proves it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.docs/one-reference-reader"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.docs-test/an-unchanged-page-writes-nothing
                      seon.docs-test/a-changed-section-rewrites-only-itself
                      seon.docs-test/a-link-to-no-section-is-refused-by-name
                      seon.docs-test/backlinks-are-a-sections-instances}}
